 The thesis set out to evaluate how far published empirical evidence supports the proposition that AI capabilities contribute to a sustainable competitive advantage in e-commerce and platform-based businesses through dynamic capabilities. Chapter~\ref{ch:intro} argued that this question matters because AI tools are becoming widely accessible, so that any lasting advantage is more likely to depend on how firms organise around AI than on the technology itself. Building on the resource-based view and dynamic capabilities theory, Chapter~\ref{ch:litreview} developed a conceptual model in which AI capability relates to sustainable competitive advantage directly (H1), relates to dynamic capabilities (H2), and works through sensing, seizing and transforming individually (H3 to H5) and through dynamic capabilities as a whole (H6). Chapter~\ref{ch:method} described a structured literature review with explicit criteria, quality appraisal and decision rules to assess this model.
 
-The review in Chapter~\ref{ch:analysis} identified 1,807 records and included 120 studies. Its findings can be condensed into four key messages. First, the literature is new and growing fast: 112 of the included studies were published in 2024 or later. Second, AI is consistently associated with competitive advantage, but mainly indirectly. In the strongest studies, the direct effect of AI disappears once other capabilities, such as agility, innovation capability or organisational learning, are included in the model \parencite{srahin2026, texeira2026}. Third, the specific mechanism proposed in this thesis has hardly been tested. Only 10 of the 120 studies measure the dimensions of dynamic capabilities, and no study estimates separate indirect effects through sensing, seizing and transforming on competitive advantage. Fourth, the evidence from e-commerce and platform-based business is very thin: only seven studies concern this context, and only one of them is of high quality \parencite{guo2025}.
